[Music]  artist bisa butler has been called a  modern day griot but instead of using  words to tell stories she uses stitches  and cloth her quilts have graced the  covers of magazines and she created the  striking illustration for the soon to be  released book unbound the memoir of  activist and metoo movement founder  tarana burke nancy chen spoke to butler  about creating a new narrative using  fabric  her vividly detailed portraits often  reimagine the lives of those they  portray  and now beast of butler's life itself is  launching in a new direction  seeing my artwork at home and in my  studio is one thing but seeing it in  this location is just  completely mind-blowing just four years  past her career as a high school art  teacher butler has unveiled her first  show at a major museum and it's at one  of the world's most renowned the art  institute of chicago how would you  describe your art when i first started  making quilts  they were not considered  fine art they were considered craft i  call my work now portrait quilts every  fiber of her life-size portraits is  imbued with meaning from pattern african  fabrics themselves to how they're  patched together i think that  historically  quilt work  craft work had been marginalized because  it was the work of women  and it was a work of people of color it  was considered like a domestic labor  inspired by her mother's and  grandmother's dress making baller  celebrates the craft as well as the  beauty and pride of the black lives they  depict  i love the idea that the images that i'm  making  are looking back out at me  so there's like a intimacy there  and it's a confrontation too so i like  the idea that there's this dialogue  between the subjects and the observers  a conversation resurrecting stories from  the african diaspora often forgotten or  dismissed her subjects real life figures  when i find photos online  or in the databases of black people who  are unnamed  and unknown i feel like  i owe it to them to try to  sort of ascertain what was their  identity the portraits range from those  whose stories are mysterious to central  figures in american history butler  transforms a sepia tone image of harriet  tubman to this a kaleidoscope of vibrant  tones it's on display at the smithsonian  national museum of african american  history and culture  what role does color play in all this  your work is so vibrant and so bright  color is everything to me i i was born  in the 70s so  talk about color technicolor technicolor  turn up the dial  on all of the colors and then you've got  the 70s palette i use the color as a way  to communicate  if i think that somebody seemed to be  more cool more somber i might use shades  of blues and purples  if somebody appears to be like a leader  or  they appear to be more forceful i'm  going to use more intense colors  butler brings her work to life at her  home in new jersey this is where the  magic happens and we're in a dining room  in your house this is your studio this  is my studio i mean they say the kitchen  is the heart of the home  basically the kitchen is the only part  of the home that my family has access to  each figure takes about six weeks to  craft one of butler's most recent works  created over seven months is now  featured at the newark museum of art  so you're about to see the biggest piece  that i've ever made  the piece that took the longest and my  this is my baby  this is stunning  the warmth of other suns depicts black  families migrating to the north in  search of a better life but butler also  takes liberties in taking care of her  subjects  if you look at the original he doesn't  have shoes  but you gave him shoes people wanted to  travel in their very best so if he  didn't have shoes it's because they  couldn't afford them and that's  something that i'd like to give back the  portraits capture the stories of who  people are  and of who they want to be i'm surprised  by how much emotion  this evokes in me  did you want people to have that  reaction when they first see your work  i actually didn't plan on that  but i think that the nature of making  quilts  you have this blanket like object on you  all the time each bit of fabric i've  touched and stitched on so the emotions  from me  are going into the quilt itself  that purposeful intent is now focused on  her next project for the smithsonian the  harlem hell fighters who helped the  united states win world war one it's  going to feature nine soldiers  whose ages range from  19 years old to about 33 and they all  fought in an all black segregated unit  figures from the past timeless as they  come back to life in butler's hands  celebrated and honored across different  generations and backgrounds  i want people  to be able to look at my work and see  the humanity in it  and  let's say  for people who are not black to  understand that we are all human beings  and we have the same wants and desires  loves and fears and for black people i  want them to see themselves and realize  that i recognize who you are and we are  the same  for cbs this morning saturday nancy chen  chicago  powerful well bisa butler's exhibit at  the chicago art institute is open  through monday  this is her book of portraits it's  available through the uh chicago art  institute's museum shop online but you  you venture through this and it's just  an explosion of color and insight and  tales of what she's been through  in the process of making  these beautiful pieces and i just love  what she says you know this is her  moment to shine a light on people that  we still have to keep talking about  these are people these are human beings  and they have lives just like everybody  i love when nancy asks what role does  color play in all this and and beast  says color's everything to me yeah and  just the vividness of the colors in all  of these pictures absolutely beautiful  stunning and she looked like she you had  said this it looked like she could have  been  literally in her own work yeah she was  adorned yeah

